---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { stack } from "../data/site";
---

<Base
  title="Stack — Inti Cerda"
  description="Languages, backend frameworks, frontend, AI tooling and infrastructure used in production."
>
  <section class="pagehead">
    <p class="pagehead__eyebrow">Stack</p>
    <h1>Tools I have shipped with, not tools I have read about.</h1>
    <p class="pagehead__body">
      Everything listed here is running in something that has real users. Where
      a choice was load-bearing, the reason is next to it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="section__label">By layer</p>
    <div class="groups reveal">
      {
        stack.map((group) => (
          <div class="group">
            <p class="group__name">{group.name}</p>
            <ul class="tags">
              {group.items.map((item) => (
                <li class="tag">{item}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="section__label">Choices worth explaining</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card reveal">
        <h2>Local LLMs instead of an API</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          Classification runs on Ollama against hardware I control. Two reasons:
          the corpus is thousands of articles a day, so a per-token bill would
          dominate the unit economics — and compliance customers are the least
          willing people on earth to let their data leave the building.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <h2>Redis Streams instead of a queue</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          Consumer groups give each service its own cursor over the same log, so
          adding a consumer never touches the producer. Replay is free, which
          matters when a classifier changes and yesterday's articles need a
          second pass.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <h2>A database per service</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          One PostgreSQL instance, many databases, no cross-service joins. It
          costs a little duplication and buys the ability to change one
          service's schema without a meeting.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <h2>JWKS at the gateway</h2>
        <p class="card__body">
          Traefik verifies RS256 signatures against a cached key set rather than
          calling the identity provider per request. Auth stops being a
          bottleneck, and claims like plan and rate limit ride along in the
          token.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
